    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Northern Rhodesia was formed in 1911 from two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorates</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-south-africa-company">British South Africa Company</a>, which ruled it until 1 April 1924, when the British Government took over its administration.</li><li>Large-scale <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> mining on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copperbelt">Copperbelt</a> began after 1924, financed mainly from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and South Africa, and by the end of the 1930s it was booming.</li><li>Its inclusion in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federation-of-rhodesia-and-nyasaland">Federation of Rhodesia and Nyasaland</a> in 1953, against strong African opposition, hastened the campaign for majority rule that led to independence as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a> in 1964.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Northern Rhodesia is the only country to have changed its name and flag during an Olympic Games: it entered the 1964 Summer Olympics as Northern Rhodesia and left the closing ceremony on 24 October as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

